prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages classified document reading for an organization.
The people involved are analysts, officers, security admins, and contractors. One person may hold both the analyst and the officer role at the same time.
The resources are documents, dossiers, and access badges. Every document records its owner, its classification, its compartments, and its status.
Each requester carries their clearance, compartments, and nationality.
Each request carries whether it comes from the intranet and the current time.
When a document carries the relevant compartment label, an analyst or officer may read it only if their clearance dominates the document's classification.
When a document does not carry that compartment label, this classification comparison does not block the analyst or officer from reading it.
An analyst or officer may read a document only when the request comes from the intranet.
Neither an analyst nor an officer may read the document from outside the intranet.
A request by an analyst to read a document that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity User {
    isAnalyst: Bool,
    isOfficer: Bool,
    isSecurityAdmin: Bool,
    isContractor: Bool,
    clearance: Long,
    compartments: Set<String>,
    nationality: String,
};

entity Document {
    owner: User,
    classification: Long,
    compartments: Set<String>,
    status: String,
};

entity Dossier;

entity AccessBadge;

action readDocument appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Document],
    context: {
        fromIntranet: Bool,
        currentTime: datetime,
        relevantCompartment: String,
    },
};
"
}
